WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.807 --> 00:00:18.132
everyone. I'm trying to find a place to hide this pretty emerald ring that my uncle gave me

2
00:00:18.192 --> 00:00:19.033
for my birthday.

3
00:00:19.994 --> 00:00:22.096
My brother keeps trying to steal it from me!

4
00:00:23.116 --> 00:00:27.560
I like the ring because it reminds me of an awesome landmark in Myanmar that Mr.

5
00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:28.661
Fay told us about.

6
00:00:29.722 --> 00:00:36.439
It's called the Shwedagon Pagoda, but I like to think of it as the Tower of Sparkling Jewels!

7
00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.363
It's not just a giant golden building; it's holy too.

8
00:00:42.204 --> 00:00:48.309
The Shwedagon Pagoda is actually a Buddhist shrine that's over 2,500 years old.

9
00:00:49.409 --> 00:00:50.810
What makes it so holy?

10
00:00:51.611 --> 00:00:56.034
Get this—it holds some of the Buddha's actual hair!

11
00:00:56.054 --> 00:01:01.679
First let me give you a picture of what the pagoda looks like, so you can see why

12
00:01:01.759 --> 00:01:03.700
everyone makes such a big deal about it.

13
00:01:05.341 --> 00:01:11.205
This landmark is really a complex of temples that sits high upon a hill in Yangon, the old

14
00:01:11.265 --> 00:01:12.726
capital city of Myanmar.

15
00:01:13.767 --> 00:01:18.630
The main pagoda is about one hundred meters tall and shaped like a giant cone.

16
00:01:19.751 --> 00:01:27.116
Its name, Shwedagon, means something like "golden place" because it's literally covered in gold and jewels.

17
00:01:28.224 --> 00:01:30.806
The main pagoda has ten sections or layers.

18
00:01:31.546 --> 00:01:37.269
The base of the pagoda is covered with 8,688 solid gold blocks.

19
00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:42.132
Above that are three terraces used by monks for worship and prayer.

20
00:01:43.212 --> 00:01:49.356
On top of them is a bell-shaped section, followed by something that looks like an upside-down bowl with

21
00:01:49.436 --> 00:01:50.716
golden bands above it.

22
00:01:51.857 --> 00:01:53.878
Above that are lotus petals.

23
00:01:54.559 --> 00:02:01.834
Whew! And there's still more—all of that is topped by a bud-shaped spire and a crown!

24
00:02:03.575 --> 00:02:14.879
Considered the main jewel piece, the crown is covered with 5,448 diamonds plus 2,317 other jewels like rubies

25
00:02:14.959 --> 00:02:15.940
and sapphires.

26
00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:23.683
In the center of this sparkling display is a giant emerald that reflects light at sunrise and sunset.

27
00:02:24.463 --> 00:02:26.084
I bet that's beautiful to see!

28
00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:33.282
Then there's a flag-shaped vane that reaches toward the sky and supports the final diamond bud at the

29
00:02:33.322 --> 00:02:37.143
top—a glittering 76-karat diamond.

30
00:02:38.163 --> 00:02:43.705
During the day the pagoda shines in the sun, and at night, it sparkles in the lights that

31
00:02:43.765 --> 00:02:45.085
illuminate the complex.

32
00:02:46.285 --> 00:02:52.427
The pagoda has over forty different halls and shrines, and it's filled with statues of buddhas and mythical

33
00:02:52.487 --> 00:02:55.828
creatures, bodhi trees, and beautiful flowers.

34
00:02:56.948 --> 00:03:00.151
It is also surrounded by 64 smaller pagodas.

35
00:03:02.012 --> 00:03:04.514
I think you could spend all day exploring the place.

36
00:03:05.996 --> 00:03:12.121
Oh! And there are even two "wishing" buddha statues in the pagoda, where you can go to make

37
00:03:12.161 --> 00:03:12.541
a wish!

38
00:03:13.642 --> 00:03:16.845
I wonder what I would wish for maybe a new little brother.

39
00:03:19.006 --> 00:03:19.727
Just kidding!

40
00:03:20.848 --> 00:03:25.532
Even though the pagoda looks amazing from the outside, it's not just a tourist spectacle.

41
00:03:26.484 --> 00:03:31.829
It's still a functioning temple with resident monks who meditate and pray all day long.

42
00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:37.954
And since the pagoda is a sacred place, all visitors, even those who are just there to tour

43
00:03:37.994 --> 00:03:42.498
the grounds, have to wear modest clothing and remove their shoes before entering.

44
00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:44.740
When Mr.

45
00:03:44.801 --> 00:03:48.404
Fay went, he did what most people do when they arrive at Shwedagon.

46
00:03:49.512 --> 00:03:55.675
He walked clockwise around the octagonal base of the pagoda to see the statues of buddhas where visitors

47
00:03:55.715 --> 00:03:57.816
leave offerings of flowers and incense.

48
00:03:58.937 --> 00:03:59.918
Next Mr.

49
00:03:59.958 --> 00:04:04.120
Fay went up one of the four main stairwells that lead into the pagoda.

50
00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:11.944
Each stairwell has over one hundred steps, so you need to be determined—and in shape—to reach the top!

51
00:04:13.084 --> 00:04:20.052
Mr. Fay climbed the southern stairway, which is lined with little shops selling books, charms, colorful prayer flags,

52
00:04:20.372 --> 00:04:21.133
and other things.

53
00:04:22.214 --> 00:04:25.678
He even bought a small statue of the pagoda for our classroom!

54
00:04:26.759 --> 00:04:30.042
You're probably wondering why the pagoda was built in the first place.

55
00:04:30.823 --> 00:04:34.928
Well, it's a great story that is part legend and part history.

56
00:04:36.696 --> 00:04:42.961
Legend has it that Singuttara Hill has always been a holy place because relics of three past buddhas

57
00:04:43.001 --> 00:04:46.003
were buried there thousands and thousands of years ago.

58
00:04:46.023 --> 00:04:55.009
Around 580 BC a king named Okkalapa knew that in order to keep Singuttara Hill holy, relics of

59
00:04:55.029 --> 00:04:57.371
a buddha needed to be enshrined there again.

60
00:04:58.552 --> 00:05:03.975
So he sat on the hill and prayed and meditated for new relics to be brought to his

61
00:05:04.035 --> 00:05:12.501
kingdom. At the same time, Siddhartha Gautama of India, also known as Lord Buddha, was meditating on his

62
00:05:12.581 --> 00:05:13.782
path to enlightenment.

63
00:05:14.823 --> 00:05:21.387
Coincidentally, two of King Okkalapa's subjects were out walking when they came upon Lord Buddha meditating.

64
00:05:22.388 --> 00:05:27.772
They offered him food, and in return Lord Buddha gave them eight hairs from his head.

65
00:05:29.473 --> 00:05:34.784
The two subjects rushed back to their kingdom to offer the hairs to King Okkalapa.

66
00:05:34.804 --> 00:05:39.505
The king was overjoyed and built a pagoda to enshrine these holy relics.

67
00:05:40.606 --> 00:05:43.406
This became the original Shwedagon Pagoda.

68
00:05:44.447 --> 00:05:49.968
Over the years the pagoda has been rebuilt and expanded, and kings and queens from around Asia have

69
00:05:50.008 --> 00:05:52.669
donated a countless number of jewels and gold.

70
00:05:53.709 --> 00:05:58.570
Today the pagoda attracts thousands of Buddhist pilgrims and tourists each year.

71
00:05:59.671 --> 00:06:04.032
Unfortunately the history of the pagoda includes some disasters too.

72
00:06:05.088 --> 00:06:11.093
There was a terrible fire there in 1931, and the city of Yangon has experienced at least eight

73
00:06:11.233 --> 00:06:18.139
massive earthquakes, a tidal wave, fighting during World War II, and occupation by foreign countries.

74
00:06:19.820 --> 00:06:26.085
But the Shwedagon Pagoda has miraculously survived all this and been rebuilt more beautifully each time.

75
00:06:27.246 --> 00:06:33.652
According to Buddhist tradition, pagodas generate peace and promote loving kindness in the land where they are built.

76
00:06:34.796 --> 00:06:40.678
So the Shwedagon Pagoda is really a magical place, and I hope it survives any other disasters that

77
00:06:40.738 --> 00:06:41.638
may come its way!

78
00:06:42.778 --> 00:06:47.139
In the meantime I need to find a place to hide this ring before my brother gets home

79
00:06:47.179 --> 00:06:47.760
from school.

80
00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:53.241
Maybe I'll just keep it in my pocket and hold it up to the sunlight each sunrise and

81
00:06:53.301 --> 00:06:57.982
sunset, so it can sparkle just like the emerald at the Shwedagon Pagoda.

82
00:06:59.143 --> 00:07:01.463
Oh no Here comes my brother.

83
00:07:02.304 --> 00:07:03.304
I've got to go now!

84
00:07:04.108 --> 00:07:04.969
See you next time!
